---
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
/* Read from the application's own pubspec, so the version on the download
   button cannot be a release behind. See src/lib/app.mjs. */
import { REPO, RELEASES, VERSION, APP_STORE } from '../lib/app.mjs';
/* Written by `npm run analyzer` beside the still it describes, so the facade is
   sized from the picture that exists rather than from a number typed here. */
import still from '../data/analyzer-still.json';
/* Written by `npm run flow` beside the pictures it describes, the same way and
   for the same reason as the still above — and it is also the gate. A stage
   whose photograph has not been taken on this machine has no entry here and is
   simply not drawn, so the section is short rather than broken. See
   scripts/render-flow.mjs, which says what each one needs. */
import flowShots from '../data/flow-shots.json';

/* Whether `npm run analyzer` has been run.
   `public/analyzer/` is about 4 MB of compiled Flutter and is git-ignored — so
   a plain `npm run build` needs no Flutter toolchain, and `npm run deploy`
   builds it first. Checking here means a site built without it offers the still
   and no button, rather than a button onto a 404. */
/* Resolved from the project root rather than from `import.meta.url`, which
   during the build points at the compiled module and not at this file. */
const hasDemo = existsSync(join(process.cwd(), 'public/analyzer/index.html'));

const DOCS = '/docs';

/* The signal path: three programs, one stream of audio, and the same numbers in
   all three of them.

   This is the claim the rest of the page rests on — that the meters in a DAW,
   on the desktop and on a tablet are not three implementations that happen to
   agree, but one engine read from three places. It is stated in prose further
   down, under `#reach`; here it is shown, because a reader who has to be told
   that three sets of meters agree has already been given something to doubt.

   Every plate is a photograph of the real thing, taken by `npm run flow`.
   `website/AGENTS.md` is why: a tool that publishes real numbers beside
   invented pictures has no way to notice when the drawing and the meter drift
   apart, and that is the one failure a metering tool cannot ship. */
const FLOW = [
  [
    'plugin',
    'In your DAW',
    'The plugin measures',
    `Insert the <strong>VST3</strong> or the <strong>Audio Unit</strong> on your master bus. It
     draws no meters of its own — only this panel, saying the link is live — and streams what it
     measured, along with where your transport is, to the app.`,
    `The plugin's panel, linked. A diagram across the top shows the host on the left and the
     analyzer app on the right, both ends lit, with audio and playhead running into the plugin's
     mark and a dashed line carrying the measurements out the other side; the host reads receiving
     and the app reads linked. Below it the input is 48k and 2 channels, elapsed time is one hour
     two minutes, and integrated loudness is −14.3.`,
  ],
  [
    'desktop',
    'On the desktop',
    'The app draws it',
    `The canvas you arranged, reading what arrived: every module on it showing the same instant of
     the same audio. Whether that audio came from the plugin, from an input or from a file, the
     meters cannot tell — and nothing about them has to change.`,
    `The desktop canvas, eight modules reading one instant: a LUFS meter and a set of concentric
     loudness arcs both at −9.9, a spectrum analyser, a loudness histogram running against a −14
     target line, a delivery verdict reading NOT READY with two failures and one pass, per-channel
     peak bars, a waveform, and a latched true-peak alert at −0.2.`,
  ],
  [
    'tablet',
    'Across the room',
    'A tablet reads it too',
    `The same application, on an iPad. Attach it to the plugin your session is running and it meters
     that stream over Wi-Fi — or flip <strong>PUBLISH</strong> on the desktop and it draws that
     canvas instead. Either way the layout is stored as cells on a grid rather than as fractions of
     a window, so what you built on a 32-inch monitor opens on a tablet still looking arranged on
     purpose.`,
    `The application running on an iPad in landscape, attached over Wi-Fi to the same plugin: six
     loudness readouts along the top, a LUFS meter, concentric loudness arcs reading −8.9, a
     per-channel peak meter, a delivery verdict reading NOT READY, a VU meter, a loudness histogram
     against a −14 target line, a distribution and a latched true-peak alert.`,
  ],
];

/* Only the stages whose photograph exists. Two of the three are committed; the
   tablet's needs an iPad simulator, so a machine without one builds a site that
   is complete and one plate shorter rather than a site with a hole in it. */
const flow = FLOW.filter(([id]) => flowShots[id]);

/* The catalogue. Every one of these is one ModuleFrame plus a painter in the
   application, reading the same MeterSource and repainting from the same
   clock — and every thumbnail is a photograph of the real thing, taken by
   `npm run modules`. See scripts/render-modules.mjs. */
// The fourteen modules, each with the photograph of it taken from the running
// application by `npm run modules`, and what the photograph shows. The images
// carry the app's own frame and title bar, because they *are* the app's frame
// and title bar — see scripts/render-modules.mjs.
const MODULES = [
  ['number-box', 'Number Box', 'Any single measurement, as a number.',
    'A Number Box reading −14.1 LUFS in green, on target.'],
  ['lufs-meter', 'LUFS Meter', 'Momentary and short-term loudness as bars, integrated loudness as a line.',
    'A LUFS Meter: momentary and short-term bars against a scale, reading −14.1 LUFS integrated over a 7.1 LU range.'],
  ['super-meter', 'Super Meter', 'Momentary, short-term and integrated loudness as three concentric arcs.',
    'A Super Meter: three concentric arcs around a −14.1 LUFS readout.'],
  ['digital-meter', 'Digital Meter', 'Sample peak and RMS, per channel, up to 7.1.',
    'A Digital Meter: left and right bars with peak-hold lines held near the top of the scale.'],
  ['vu-meter', 'VU Meter', 'A needle, with the ballistics of the real thing.',
    'A VU Meter reading −7.0 VU: a needle under a banded scale that turns red above 0 VU, with the peak mark left standing near the top of it.'],
  ['alert-meter', 'Alert Meter', 'One measurement, watched, with the worst it has been latched.',
    'An Alert Meter in red, showing true peak at −0.2 dBTP with −0.2 latched as the worst so far.'],
  ['validator', 'Validator', 'The delivery decision, as a table.',
    'A Validator reading NOT READY: loudness and range pass, true peak fails at −0.2 against a −1.0 ceiling.'],
  ['histogram', 'Histogram', 'Loudness against time: how the programme moved, and when it was over target.',
    'A Histogram: thirty seconds of short-term loudness against a dashed target line, with a dip where the programme breaks.'],
  ['loudness-distribution', 'Loudness Distribution', 'How much of the programme was spent at each loudness.',
    'A Loudness Distribution: a peak near −14 LUFS with the 7.9 LU range bracketed across it.'],
  ['spectrum-analyzer', 'Spectrum Analyzer', 'Level against frequency, log-spaced, with a peak hold.',
    'A Spectrum Analyzer: a bass-heavy curve falling away above 10 kHz, with a peak-hold line over it.'],
  ['spectrogram', 'Spectrogram', 'Frequency against time, with level as brightness.',
    'A Spectrogram: frequency against time, with sustained tones as horizontal bands and transients as vertical ones.'],
  ['oscilloscope', 'Oscilloscope', 'The waveform itself, one lane per channel or both overlaid.',
    'An Oscilloscope showing 20 milliseconds of waveform in two lanes, left above right.'],
  ['phase-scope', 'Phase Scope', 'A goniometer: left against right, rotated so mono stands upright.',
    'A Phase Scope: an upright figure leaning slightly, drawn from left against right.'],
  ['stereo-cloud', 'Stereo Cloud', 'Where each frequency sits in the stereo image, accumulated over time.',
    'A Stereo Cloud: frequency up the side, stereo position across, bass gathered at the centre and the top spread wide.'],
];

const METRICS = [
  [
    'Loudness',
    'LUFS, weighted the way <strong>ITU-R BS.1770-4</strong> says — the curve that makes a meter agree with your ears about what is loud instead of counting energy: low end counts for less, presence for more. Worked out for whatever sample rate your material is at, not for 48 kHz and hoped.',
  ],
  [
    'Integrated',
    'The whole programme as one number, gated to <strong>EBU R 128</strong>: passages far below the rest are left out of it, so the silence between phrases and the fade at the top do not drag the number down below what the track sounds like.',
  ],
  ['Momentary / Short-term', '400 milliseconds and 3 seconds. One is what a transient does to your loudness; the other is what a section does.'],
  [
    'LRA',
    'Loudness range: how far the loud parts of the programme sit above the quiet ones, measured across the gated short-term readings from the 10th to the 95th percentile. It is the number a broadcaster asks for and the one that says whether a master has been flattened.',
  ],
  [
    'True peak',
    'The peak <em>between</em> samples as well as on them, found by oversampling four times as <strong>BS.1770-4</strong> requires. It is the number a delivery ceiling means, and it sits above the sample peak your DAW shows — by as much as a dB and a half on heavily limited material.',
  ],
  [
    'Spectrum',
    '512 bands, spaced the way you hear rather than the way an FFT arrives. Each band shows the <strong>loudest</strong> content inside it rather than the average, so a narrow resonance stands at its real height instead of being smoothed into the noise beside it.',
  ],
  ['Correlation', 'How alike the two channels are: +1 is mono, 0 is uncorrelated, −1 is one channel inverted against the other.'],
  [
    'Crest',
    'Peak minus RMS: how much room the transients have above the body of the sound. A sine is 3.01 dB, a snare is a lot more, and a master that has been squeezed trends towards nothing.',
  ],
  [
    'Clip',
    'The longest run of samples sitting at full scale since you last reset, per channel — and it is latched, so a clip three samples long is still there when you come back to look for it.',
  ],
];

const GAPS = [
  [
    'Loudness is measured continuously, and that is the only mode.',
    'There is no Elapsed mode that runs from where you pressed play, and no Timecode mode that measures a region you mark out. <strong>Continuous is what every module shows today</strong> — and it is the mode you can honour without a playhead at all, which is what a delivery target asks for anyway.',
  ],
  [
    'The plugin draws nothing, and there is no Pro Tools version.',
    'It measures your DAW’s audio and sends it across; the app is what draws it, so the app has to be running beside your session. It ships as <strong>VST3 and Audio Unit</strong>, which between them reach every other DAW people master in — AAX needs Avid’s SDK and a registered developer account, and neither is something a free project can promise you.',
  ],
  [
    'Metering your computer’s own output takes all of it.',
    'There is no per-application selection on any platform — you get everything going to your speakers, so mute what you are not measuring. On macOS the first attempt asks permission, and <strong>a refusal is silent</strong>: the meters read digital black rather than saying no. If they sit at the floor with something obviously playing, look in Privacy &amp; Security.',
  ],
  [
    'Offline analysis does not read Ogg Vorbis, Opus, AAC or ALAC.',
    'WAV, AIFF, RF64, Wave64, FLAC and MP3 cover what a master is delivered <em>as</em>, which is what a delivery check is for. The rest is what a master is distributed as after somebody else transcodes it. Nothing is quietly half-measured in the meantime: a file it cannot read is refused.',
  ],
  [
    'A file is measured whole, from the start.',
    'There is no region selection and no seeking while a file is analysed, because an integrated loudness taken over a file somebody skipped through is a measurement of a programme nobody played.',
  ],
  [
    'The tablet link has no password.',
    'Anyone who can reach the port can read your meters and your layout — not your audio, which never goes on the wire, and nothing on the tablet can change anything on the desktop. Publishing is off at every launch on purpose and is switched on from the menu bar, where you can see that it is on. <strong>Do not switch it on at a venue whose Wi-Fi you do not control.</strong>',
  ],
];

const PLATFORMS = [
  ['macOS 14.2+', 'Universal — Apple silicon and Intel', '.pkg', 'VST3 + AU'],
  ['Windows 10 1809+', 'Uninstaller in Installed apps', '.exe', 'VST3'],
  ['Linux', 'install.sh, no root', '.tar.gz', 'VST3'],
  ['Linux', 'One file, no root, GTK from the host', '.AppImage', '—'],
  ['Linux', 'Sandboxed, carries its own runtime', '.flatpak', '—'],
  ['Any', 'The file analyser, for a terminal', 'oaa CLI', '—'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Open Audio Analyzer — free and open-source loudness and spectrum analyzer"
  description="A free and open-source loudness and spectrum analyzer. Fourteen meters on a canvas you arrange, measuring LUFS and true peak to EBU R128 — desktop, DAW, tablet."
  path="/"
>
  <!-- ===================== hero ===================== -->
  <!--
    The first screen is the instrument, not a description of it.

    It was a column of prose beside a five-row table of platforms and licences.
    The table was the problem: everything on it is true, none of it is what
    somebody wants in the first ten seconds, and it is all stated properly in
    the download section further down — where a table with four columns can
    actually say which installer carries the plugin. It has become one line,
    and the space it held is now the thing this project makes.

    The live analyzer used to be a section of its own under a heading. It is
    here instead, so that the page opens on a canvas of working meters with the
    real one a press away, and so that the reader passes one screen to reach it
    rather than three.
  -->
  <section class="hero shell" id="analyzer">
    <p class="eyebrow num">
      <a class="link-quiet" href={REPO} rel="noopener">github.com/JonasGrunau/open_audio_analyzer</a>
    </p>

    <h1>Open Audio Analyzer</h1>

    <div class="hero-lede">
      <p class="prose hero-deck">
        A free and open-source loudness and spectrum analyzer. Fourteen meter modules on a canvas
        you arrange — on the desktop, inside your DAW, and on a tablet beside the desk.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!--
      The button row sits outside `.hero-lede` on purpose. That box is
      `max-width: 62ch`, which is the measure the deck is set to and the reason
      the sentence above reads as a statement; a row of controls is not prose
      and inherited nothing from it but a wrap. Three buttons and two store
      badges are about 850 px, so inside the measure the badges fell to a second
      line at every window width there is.

      Apple's badge is first because Apple's guidelines say it is — "Place the
      App Store badge first in the lineup of badges" — and Google's ask only
      that theirs be "the same size or larger than the other badges", which
      equal height satisfies. Both artworks are used exactly as their owners
      publish them: the black badge with its grey border, no recolouring, no
      hover transform, and nothing typeset inside the quarter-of-a-badge-height
      of clear space each of them requires. That is what `--s12` under a 48 px
      badge is measuring out, and it is why the captions are a gap rather than
      an overlay.

      The Play badge points at `/testing`, not at Play. Both of the URLs Play
      offers for a closed test turn away everybody who is not already on the
      tester list, and neither says why — so the badge would recruit nobody.
      Google's badge guidelines constrain the artwork and say nothing about the
      destination, which was checked; Apple's do constrain it, which is one more
      reason their badge is not a link here.
    -->
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={RELEASES} rel="noopener">Download {VERSION}</a>
      <a class="btn" href={REPO} rel="noopener">Source</a>
      <a class="btn" href={DOCS}>Documentation</a>

      <div class="stores">
        <div class="store">
          {APP_STORE ? (
            <a class="store-badge" href={APP_STORE} rel="noopener">
              <img
                class="store-art"
                src="/badges/app-store.svg"
                alt="Download on the App Store"
                width="144"
                height="48"
                decoding="async"
              />
            </a>
          ) : (
            <span class="store-badge">
              <img
                class="store-art"
                src="/badges/app-store.svg"
                alt="Download on the App Store"
                width="144"
                height="48"
                decoding="async"
              />
            </span>
          )}
        </div>

        <div class="store">
          <a class="store-badge" href="/testing">
            <img
              class="store-art"
              src="/badges/google-play.svg"
              alt="Get it on Google Play"
              width="162"
              height="48"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- What the plate said, in the space of a line. Anything a reader has to
         act on — which installer carries the plugin, what each one will not
         do — is in the download section, which has room to say it. -->
    <p class="hero-meta">
      <span class="num">macOS · Windows · Linux · iPadOS · Android</span>
      <span class="hero-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="num">VST3 · Audio Unit · CLI</span>
      <span class="hero-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="num">Free, GPL-3.0</span>
    </p>

    <figure class="facade" data-facade>
      {/* The one picture on the page that is fetched before anything is
          scrolled, so the one whose size decides Largest Contentful Paint.

          `sizes` is not a guess — it is `.shell` written out. That box is
          `min(100vw, 1440px)` wide less a gutter of 32 px, or 24 below 900 px,
          or 16 below 560, and those three breakpoints are in global.css. Get it
          wrong in the optimistic direction and every phone downloads the
          desktop file while the browser reports no problem at all; the widths
          themselves are listed in scripts/render-analyzer.mjs, which writes
          them. */}
      <img
        src="/analyzer-still.webp"
        srcset={still.sources.map((source) => `${source.file} ${source.width}w`).join(', ')}
        sizes="(min-width: 1440px) 1376px, (min-width: 901px) calc(100vw - 64px), (min-width: 561px) calc(100vw - 48px), calc(100vw - 32px)"
        alt="Eight meter modules on one canvas: loudness bars, three concentric arcs, a spectrum analyser, a minute of loudness against time, a delivery verdict reading NOT READY, per-channel peak bars, a waveform and a latched true-peak alert."
        width={still.width}
        height={still.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
        decoding="async"
      />
      {
        hasDemo && (
          <>
            {/* Three elements where one glyph would do, because each carries a
                transform of its own once the control is pressed: the mark
                travels to the middle of the picture, the ring inside it turns
                into a spinner and rotates, and the triangle fades out from
                under both. One element can only carry one transform. */}
            <button class="facade-go" type="button" data-facade-go>
              <span class="facade-go-mark" aria-hidden="true" data-facade-mark>
                <span class="facade-go-ring" />
                {/* Drawn, not typed. U+25B6 BLACK RIGHT-POINTING TRIANGLE is on
                    the emoji tables with a *text* default presentation, which a
                    desktop honours and a phone does not: iOS and Android both
                    resolve it out of the colour emoji font, so the hairline
                    square came out holding a rounded, oversized emoji play
                    button. A path has no font behind it to be substituted. */}
                <svg class="facade-go-tri" viewBox="0 0 10 12" aria-hidden="true">
                  <path d="M0 0 L10 6 L0 12 Z" />
                </svg>
              </span>
              <span class="facade-go-text" data-facade-text>
                <span data-facade-label>Load the live analyzer</span>
                <span class="facade-go-sub">Real meters, running in your browser</span>
              </span>
            </button>
            {/* The spinner says nothing to a screen reader, and the label it
                replaces is on its way off the picture. This is what announces
                the wait, and the end of it. */}
            <p class="visually-hidden" role="status" data-facade-status />
          </>
        )
      }
    </figure>

    <!-- Required, not decorative. The programme behind those meters is a CC BY
         3.0 track, and that licence asks for credit wherever the audio is
         published — which a page that plays it is. The wording and the link are
         copied from test_audio/ATTRIBUTION.md, written by the tool that fetched
         it, so there is one place this is stated from.

         It is not drawn on a phone, where the control that loads the analyzer is
         not drawn either: with nothing to press, the audio is never fetched and
         never played, and what is left on the page is a photograph of meter
         readings. Those are measurements of the track, not the track. The two go
         together and must keep going together — a phone that can start the
         analyzer is a phone that has to carry this line. See the media query in
         the stylesheet. -->
    <p class="hero-credit">
      Measured from <em>Apathy And Resignation Are The Sources Of Our Misfortune</em> by
      <a class="link-quiet" href="https://citizensoftheempire.bandcamp.com/" rel="noopener">Citizens
      Of The Empire</a>, <a class="link-quiet" href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0" rel="noopener">CC
      BY 3.0</a>. The readings are the engine's own, recorded from that track — not a simulation.
    </p>

  </section>

  <!-- ===================== the signal path ===================== -->
  <section class="section shell" id="workflow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">How it works</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">One stream of audio,<br />measured once</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="prose flow-intro">
      Nothing along this path measures anything twice. The plugin takes the readings, the desktop
      draws them, and a tablet across the room draws the same ones — one engine, one set of
      numbers, three places to read them. So the meter you glance at from the far side of the studio
      cannot quietly disagree with the one under your hand.
    </p>

    <ol class="flow" style={`--flow-n: ${flow.length}`}>
      {
        flow.map(([id, where, name, body, alt], i) => (
          <li class="flow-step">
            <p class="flow-mark">
              <span class="flow-n num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <span class="flow-where">{where}</span>
            </p>
            {/* The tablet is the one plate that is a picture of an object rather
                than a picture of a screen, so it is the one that gets a bezel.
                See the note on the radius in the stylesheet. */}
            <figure class={id === 'tablet' ? 'flow-plate flow-device' : 'flow-plate'}>
              <img
                src={`/flow/${id}.webp`}
                alt={alt}
                width={flowShots[id].width}
                height={flowShots[id].height}
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
                sizes="(min-width: 1081px) 30vw, (min-width: 561px) calc(100vw - 48px), calc(100vw - 32px)"
              />
            </figure>
            <h3 class="flow-name">{name}</h3>
            <p class="prose flow-body" set:html={body} />
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell" id="modules">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Modules</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Fourteen meter modules</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="prose mod-intro">
      Fourteen of them, and you choose which ones are on screen. Drag a meter onto the canvas, size
      it, put it where you want it: loudness and a delivery verdict when you are finishing a master,
      a spectrum and a phase scope while you mix, a VU and a waveform while you track. They are all
      reading the same audio at the same instant, so two meters showing the same thing never
      disagree with each other — and the arrangement you settle on is saved as a preset, for the
      desktop and for the tablet beside it.
    </p>

    <ul class="cat">
      {
        MODULES.map(([shot, name, desc, alt]) => (
          <li class="cat-item">
            <div class="cat-shot">
              <img
                src={`/modules/${shot}.webp`}
                alt={alt}
                width="390"
                height="256"
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
              />
            </div>
            <h3 class="cat-name">{name}</h3>
            <p class="cat-desc">{desc}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== measurement ===================== -->
  <section class="section shell" id="measurement">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Measurement</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Every number, and what<br />it actually means</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="split">
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Measurement definitions">
      <table class="spec">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Reading</th>
            <th scope="col">What it is</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            METRICS.map(([q, d]) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="q">{q}</td>
                <td set:html={d} />
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>

      <aside class="gate module">
        <div class="module-bar">
          <span class="name">Checked against the reference cases</span>
        </div>
        <div class="gate-body">
          <ul class="gate-list">
            <li>
              <span class="gate-k">EBU Tech 3341 — loudness</span>
              <span class="gate-v sig">PASS</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="gate-k">EBU Tech 3342 — loudness range</span>
              <span class="gate-v sig">PASS</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="gate-k">A full-scale tone reads</span>
              <span class="gate-v sig">0.0 dBFS</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="gate-k">44.1 / 48 / 88.2 / 96 / 192 kHz</span>
              <span class="gate-v sig">AGREE</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="gate-k">The same track, live and from a file</span>
              <span class="gate-v sig">IDENTICAL</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="gate-note">
            These run on all three desktop platforms before any change is accepted, and the full
            official EBU and ITU vector sets are run by hand on top of them. <strong>A loudness
            meter nobody has held against the reference cases is a number generator</strong> — the
            EBU publishes them for exactly this, and a delivery decision is made from what comes
            out.
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== honesty ===================== -->
  <section class="section shell honesty">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Dynamics</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Dynamic range, and<br />which one it means</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="dyn">
      <p class="prose">
        There is no standard that says what “dynamic range” is, so this says exactly which
        subtraction it means. <code>DR-S</code> is the true peak against the short-term loudness:
        how much room the loudest moment has over how loud that passage is. <code>DR-I</code> is the
        same question about the whole track — the highest true peak against the integrated loudness.
        Watch either one fall as you push a limiter and you are watching the squeeze happen.
      </p>
      <div class="stack-24">
        <p class="prose">
          <code>PSR</code> and <code>PLR</code> are those same two numbers under the names you will
          have met elsewhere, and both are shown — <strong>four names for two numbers</strong>, each
          worked out once so they cannot end up disagreeing, and printed once in a report rather
          than twice under different headings.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="link" href={`${DOCS}/metrics`}>Read the metrics document</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== reach ===================== -->
  <section class="section shell" id="reach">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Plugin, tablet and CLI</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">The same engine,<br />in three more places</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="reach">
      <article class="stack-16">
        <h3>In a DAW</h3>
        <p class="prose reach-p">
          A <strong>VST3</strong> and an <strong>Audio Unit</strong>, on a track, a bus or the
          master. What you read while mixing is not a second set of meters built to look like the
          first — it is the first, watching your session.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="stack-16">
        <h3>On a tablet</h3>
        <p class="prose reach-p">
          It finds the host by itself, or reads a pairing code off the desktop’s screen with its
          camera, or takes an address typed by hand — <strong>three routes, because the first one is
          what a venue’s Wi-Fi blocks.</strong>
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="stack-16">
        <h3>On the command line</h3>
        <p class="prose reach-p">
          <code>oaa</code> measures a file without opening anything — one track, or every file in an
          album folder in one go — and prints a report you can read, or a CSV you can open in a
          spreadsheet. It runs the <em>same</em> analysis the app runs on live audio, down to the
          last decimal, and nothing is resampled or remixed on the way in: a converter in front of a
          meter changes what the meter says.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="term module">
      <div class="module-bar">
        <span class="name">oaa — the command-line analyser</span>
      </div>
      <pre class="term-body"><code><span class="c">$</span> oaa master.wav                              <span class="cm"># the whole report, readable</span>
<span class="c">$</span> oaa --target streaming-14 master.wav        <span class="cm"># …and does it pass?</span>
<span class="c">$</span> oaa --target streaming-14 album/*.wav       <span class="cm"># the whole album, one pass each</span>
<span class="c">$</span> oaa --format csv -o loudness.csv master.wav  <span class="cm"># loudness over time, for a spreadsheet</span>
<span class="c">$</span> oaa --list-targets                          <span class="cm"># what you can measure against</span></code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== configuration ===================== -->
  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Configuration</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Settings, presets, targets<br />and skins</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cfg">
      <div class="stack-24">
        <p class="prose">
          Everything you set up is yours to keep. Save an arrangement of meters as a preset and it
          opens that way next time — the same modules in the same places, on a 32-inch monitor or on
          the tablet beside it. Presets, delivery targets and colour schemes are small text files in
          a folder you can open, so you can carry your setup to the studio machine, put it somewhere
          the whole room reads it from, or send it to somebody who works the way you do.
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          A <strong>delivery target</strong> is simply the numbers you have to hit: a loudness, how
          far either side of it you are allowed to land, a true-peak ceiling, and a limit on how much
          the loudness may move across the track. The streaming and broadcast ones come with the app.
          If your label, your client or your own ears say something else, write your own — and if you
          give it the same name as one that ships, <strong>yours replaces it everywhere</strong>: in
          presets that already ask for it, and in the pass or fail the command-line analyser reports.
          Delete your file and the original is back.
        </p>
        <p class="prose">
          Colour works the same way. Thirteen things on screen have a colour of their own — the
          meter track, the readout, the colour that means <em>over the limit</em> — and a scheme
          names as many of them as it likes and takes the rest as they were, so changing one colour
          is a three-line file. There is a picker for each of them: the canvas repaints while you
          drag, any tablet mirroring the session repaints with it, and each colour tells you how well
          it reads against the panel behind it — so you find out that your meter track is nearly
          invisible before you are on a dark stage looking for it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="json module">
        <div class="module-bar">
          <span class="name">calibrations/house-standard.json</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="json-body"><code>&lbrace;
  <span class="k">"id"</span>: <span class="s">"house-standard"</span>,
  <span class="k">"name"</span>: <span class="s">"House standard"</span>,
  <span class="k">"lufs_target"</span>: <span class="n">-12.0</span>,
  <span class="k">"lufs_tolerance"</span>: <span class="n">0.5</span>,
  <span class="k">"true_peak_max"</span>: <span class="n">-1.0</span>,
  <span class="k">"lra_max"</span>: <span class="n">14.0</span>,
  <span class="k">"vu_reference"</span>: <span class="n">-18.0</span>
&rbrace;</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== known gaps ===================== -->
  <section class="section shell" id="gaps">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Known gaps</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">What is not built</h2>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="prose gaps-intro">
      Six things you are likely to meet, said plainly rather than left for you to find. There are
      twelve in all — the other six are in the README, and most of them only mean something to
      somebody working on the code.
    </p>

    <ul class="gaps">
      {
        GAPS.map(([head, body]) => (
