<style>
  /* ---------- hero ---------- */

  .hero {
    padding-block: var(--s48) var(--s96);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  h1 {
    margin-top: var(--s24);
  }

  /* One column, and narrow. The deck is the only prose on this screen, and a
     sentence set the width of a 1440 px page is a sentence nobody finishes —
     the measure is what makes it read as a statement rather than a paragraph. */
  .hero-lede {
    margin-top: var(--s32);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .hero-deck {
    font-size: clamp(1.1875rem, 1.5vw, 1.4375rem);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text);
    max-width: none;
  }

  .cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    /* The badges are taller than the buttons, because each one carries a caption
       under it. `flex-start` is what keeps the two badge artworks on the same
       baseline as the three buttons and lets the captions hang into the space
       below, which was empty anyway. `center` would push the buttons down by
       half a caption and align nothing to anything. */
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--s12);
    margin-top: var(--s32);
  }

  /* ---------- the two app store badges ----------
     Vendor artwork. The CSS here only ever positions it, with **one deliberate
     exception** recorded below: no recolouring, no filter, no transform, no
     hover state and no animation on `.store-art`, because Google's guidelines
     forbid altering the badge and Apple's forbid animating it as well.

     The exception is the dimming of the App Store badge while there is no iPad
     app to download, which is a departure from Apple's "Don't modify … the App
     Store badge" made knowingly and at the maintainer's instruction. It is
     scoped as tightly as it can be — see `span.store-badge` — so it applies to
     the pending badge and to nothing else, and it disappears by itself the day
     `APP_STORE` gets a URL. Do not widen it, and do not take it as licence to
     restyle the live badge. */
  .stores {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    /* Google asks for one quarter of the badge height as clear space, which is
       12 px under a 48 px badge; Apple asks for the same quarter. `--s16`
       between the two badges clears both with room to spare. */
    gap: var(--s16);
    /* The hairline is the seam between what this project publishes and what a
       store does. It is drawn on the left of the group rather than as an
       element of its own so that it wraps with the badges instead of stranding
       itself at the end of the button row. */
    position: relative;
    margin-left: var(--s12);
    padding-left: var(--s24);
  }

  .stores::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 1px;
    /* The button height, not the group's: the seam belongs to the row the
       badges are in line with, and running it down past the captions would make
       it the tallest mark on the screen. */
    height: var(--s48);
    background: var(--hair-strong);
  }

  .store {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    /* The clear space itself. Both guidelines say no typography inside a
       quarter of the badge height, and this is that quarter measured out. */
    gap: var(--s12);
  }

  /* The link is the badge and only the badge. The caption below is a subline
     about the badge, not a second half of it, so it sits outside the anchor —
     otherwise the click target is an L-shape that reaches down into the clear
     space, and the caption reads as a label the artwork is missing. */
  .store-badge {
    display: block;
  }

  .store-art {
    display: block;
    height: var(--s48);
    /* Width follows the artwork's own aspect ratio. Never a fixed width — both
       guidelines forbid stretching the badge, and the two of them are not the
       same shape: 3.37:1 for Google Play against 2.99:1 for the App Store. */
    width: auto;
  }

  /* The badge that is not a link yet, greyed so the pending state is visible
     rather than only readable. A `<span>` rather than an `<a>` is exactly the
     condition — `index.astro` renders the anchor the moment `APP_STORE` is set
     — so there is no class to remember to remove and no way for this to outlive
     the thing it describes.

     Opacity rather than a filter: the badge is already monochrome, so
     `grayscale()` would do nothing, and blending it toward the page background
     is what "not yet" looks like on a dark ground. It is the only thing that
     says so now — the caption that used to spell it out is gone — which is why
     it stays.

     See the block header for why this one property is here at all. */
  span.store-badge .store-art {
    opacity: 0.42;
  }

  /* Keyboard focus lands on the badge, so it has to be visible there. An
     outline is drawn outside the element and disappears with the focus, which
     is why it is the one mark allowed to cross into the clear space — the
     alternative is a link nobody navigating by keyboard can find. */
  a.store-badge:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--signal);
    outline-offset: var(--s4);
  }

  /* Where the five-row plate went: platforms, what it loads into, what it
     costs — as one readout line, in the meter face and at the size a module
     draws its own readout, because that is what it is. */
  .hero-meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--s12);
    margin-top: var(--s32);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--faint);
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  .hero-sep {
    color: var(--hair-strong);
  }

  /* Under the canvas, quiet, and never removed: see the note in the markup. */
  .hero-credit {
    margin-top: var(--s16);
    max-width: 78ch;
    font-family: var(--prose);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .hero-credit em {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* The two links in the credit are the only ones on the site that sit *inside*
     a run of prose, and `.link-quiet` distinguishes itself from the text around
     it by colour alone — `--muted` against `--faint`, which is 1.2:1 between
     them. Colour at that distance is not a difference a reader with low vision
     or a monochrome display can see, so the link needs a second cue: the same
     hairline underline `.link` carries. Everywhere else `.link-quiet` is a whole
     paragraph or a whole cell, where there is no surrounding text to be
     confused with, and it stays bare. */
  .hero-credit .link-quiet {
    border-bottom-color: var(--faint);
  }

  .hero-credit .link-quiet:hover {
    border-bottom-color: var(--signal);
  }

  /* ---------- the live analyzer in the hero, behind a facade ---------- */

  /* A photograph of the real canvas, with the real thing one press away.
     The aspect ratio is the still's own, declared so that nothing moves when
     the image arrives or when the iframe replaces it. */
  /* No aspect-ratio here: the still is in normal flow and its own width and
     height attributes give the box its shape, so a re-shot still of a different
     size cannot leave a strip of background down one edge. The iframe is laid
     over it. */
  .facade {
    position: relative;
    margin: var(--s32) 0 0;
    background: var(--ink);
    border: 1px solid var(--hair);
    overflow: hidden;
    line-height: 0;
  }

  .facade img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .facade img,
  .facade iframe {
    display: block;
    border: 0;
  }

  /* Sits over the still rather than beside it, because the still is the button:
     the thing you press is a picture of what you get. */
  .facade-go {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--s12);
    width: 100%;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 38%, transparent);
    border: 0;
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      background 120ms linear,
      opacity 320ms ease;
  }

  /* `:hover` still matches a disabled button, and the control disables itself
     the moment it is pressed — so the resting brightening has to be gated, or
     the panel changes tone under a pointer that is only resting on a spinner. */
  .facade-go:not(:disabled):hover,
  .facade-go:not(:disabled):focus-visible {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 22%, transparent);
  }

  .facade-go:disabled {
    cursor: default;
  }

  /* The mark holds the position and travels; the ring holds the shape and
     spins; the triangle is what goes. Stacked above the label so that the
     label passes behind it rather than through it. */
  .facade-go-mark {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 2.75rem;
    height: 2.75rem;
    flex: none;
  }

  .facade-go-ring {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--signal);
    border-radius: 0;
    /* The fill is the surface the label goes behind: a hairline outline hides
       nothing, and the last few pixels of a letter are under the disc before
       the cut reaches them. */
    background: transparent;
    /* All three land together at 200 ms, which is where the spin picks up. The
       radius is not on an ease-out: a curve that is ninety per cent round in a
       fifth of the time makes a shape that is visibly a circle long before it
       is allowed to turn, which is the wait this animation exists to deny. */
    transition:
      border-radius 200ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1),
      background-color 200ms linear,
      border-color 140ms linear 60ms;
  }

  .facade-go-tri {
    width: 0.625rem;
    height: 0.75rem;
    fill: var(--signal);
    /* A right-pointing triangle carries its weight left of the box that holds
       it, so a centred box leaves it looking adrift. A margin and not a
       transform: the transform belongs to the animation below. */
    margin-left: 2px;
    /* Gone before the square is a circle. A play mark inside a spinner is a
       control offering to start something that has started. */
    transition:
      opacity 120ms linear,
      transform 200ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1);
  }

  .facade-go-text {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* A second line on the control, saying what pressing it gets you. */
  .facade-go-sub {
    font-family: var(--readout);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  /* ---- pressed: the two halves converge and the mark becomes a spinner ----

     The mark aims at the middle of the control; the label aims at the mark, and
     goes behind it. Along whichever axis the two sit on — which is what makes
     the same three rules carry the column layout below, where the label is under
     the mark rather than beside it. The distances are pixels measured at the
     press and written onto the elements as `--dx` / `--dy`, and the cut that
     hides the label as `--c*` / `--s*`; see `src/scripts/facade.js` for what
     each one is and why they are measured from `offsetLeft` rather than from a
     bounding box. */
  .facade-go.is-loading .facade-go-mark {
    animation: facade-converge 640ms both;
  }

  .facade-go.is-loading .facade-go-text {
    animation: facade-swallow 640ms both;
  }

  .facade-go.is-loading .facade-go-tri {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: scale(0.5);
  }

  .facade-go.is-loading .facade-go-ring {
    border-radius: 50%;
    /* Near enough to solid. The cut that hides the label sits on the circle's
       outline where the outline crosses the label's corner, so the middle rows
       of a letter spend nine pixels *under* the disc before they reach it — at
       72 % they went through it as a ghost, which is exactly the overlay this
       stopped being. */
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 94%, transparent);
    /* Two sides of four is half a circle: the arc that reads as motion, over a
       track faint enough to still read as the box it came from. */
    border-color: var(--signal) var(--signal) color-mix(in srgb, var(--signal) 14%, transparent)
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--signal) 14%, transparent);
    /* Delayed by exactly as long as the shape takes, and not a frame longer. A
       spinner that has finished becoming a spinner and is waiting to reach the
       middle of the picture before it turns is a stall in the one place the
       animation is there to say the opposite; it turns on its way across. */
    animation: facade-spin 900ms linear 200ms infinite;
  }

  /* Loaded. The whole control goes, spinner and scrim together, over the same
     320 ms the frame underneath takes to arrive. */
  .facade-go.is-done {
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  @keyframes facade-converge {
    0% {
      transform: translate(0, 0);
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.6, 1);
    }
    /* A short pull the other way first. It costs four per cent of the distance
       and is the difference between a thing that slides and a thing that goes. */
    20% {
      transform: translate(calc(var(--dx, 0px) * -0.07), calc(var(--dy, 0px) * -0.07));
      /* Not an ease-out. A curve that only decelerates puts nine tenths of the
         distance into the first third of the time and then coasts, which reads
         as a snap followed by nothing; this one takes the weight up and sets it
         down. */
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.12, 1);
    }
    100% {
      transform: translate(var(--dx, 0px), var(--dy, 0px));
    }
  }

  /* The label does not fade and does not shrink. It slides into the disc and is
     cut off at the disc's edge, which is what "behind" means: the same three
     stops as the converge above, so that the cut — an inset that eats the label
     from the side it is travelling towards — stays on an edge that is itself
     moving. `--c*` is where that cut starts inside the label, negative for as
     long as the disc has not reached it, which is what the `max()` is for; `--s*`
     is how far it sweeps, and by the last stop it has swept the whole label. */
  @keyframes facade-swallow {
    0% {
      transform: translate(0, 0);
      clip-path: inset(max(0px, var(--ct, 0px)) 0 0 max(0px, var(--cl, 0px)));
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.6, 1);
    }
    20% {
      transform: translate(calc(var(--dx, 0px) * -0.07), calc(var(--dy, 0px) * -0.07));
      clip-path: inset(
        max(0px, calc(var(--ct, 0px) + var(--st, 0px) * -0.07))
          max(0px, calc(var(--sr, 0px) * -0.07)) 0
          max(0px, calc(var(--cl, 0px) + var(--sl, 0px) * -0.07))
      );
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.12, 1);
    }
    /* Two frames of opacity at the very end, where the label is already inside
       the disc and cannot be seen. It is not part of the effect — it is what
       keeps a browser that could not compute the cut from leaving the label
       lying across the picture for the whole of the wait. */
    98% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    100% {
      transform: translate(var(--dx, 0px), var(--dy, 0px));
      clip-path: inset(
        max(0px, calc(var(--ct, 0px) + var(--st, 0px))) max(0px, var(--sr, 0px)) 0
          max(0px, calc(var(--cl, 0px) + var(--sl, 0px)))
      );
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  @keyframes facade-spin {
    to {
      transform: rotate(1turn);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    /* At this width the label and the cost together are wider than the button,
       and the mark alone says enough. */
    .facade-go {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--s8);
      padding: var(--s16);
    }
    .facade-go-text {
      align-items: center;
      text-align: center;
    }
  }

  /* ---- on a phone the still is a photograph and nothing more ----

     Eight dense meters scaled to a 390 px screen are not legible; the
     application behind the control is two megabytes of compiled Flutter over
     whatever connection a phone happens to have; and an iframe that takes touch
     inside a document you are scrolling fights the scroll for every gesture. So
     there is nothing to press here, and the credit under the picture goes with
     the control — it is required of a page that *publishes* the programme, which
     is what pressing that control makes this one. See the note in the markup.

     Both orientations, which is what the second query is for: a phone on its
     side is 844 px wide and 390 px tall, so width alone would hand it the
     control its portrait screen was refused. The coarse pointer is what keeps a
     merely short desktop window out. A tablet is above both and still gets the
     real thing — it is a platform this application ships on. */
  @media (max-width: 720px), (max-height: 480px) and (pointer: coarse) {
    .facade-go,
    .hero-credit {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* Three across, not four. A meter is a dense picture and fourteen of them at
     a quarter of the page each is a contact sheet — at a third, the scale
     labels and the readouts inside them are legible without opening anything.
     The photographs are captured at four device pixels per logical one, which
     is what keeps them sharp at this size. */
  .cat {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s32);
